ss transport, controlling allocation effects without numerical objective changes.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"607_episode10_point_last_tile256\",\n    \"sha256\": \"196650895080394f58b65063694b837128df266424028a8aad1aa241d6913d66\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Block final reference-only point power and column marginal into cache tiles, using new two-dimensional GEMM reductions. No row sums computed at final step.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"608_episode10_point_last_tile512\",\n    \"sha256\": \"6de827023bef3d86f1f88d27c2a8c6c78b5fd909ef7beceb2b94f761da6584c5\",\n    \"hypothesis\": \"Block final reference-only point power and column marginal into cache tiles, using new two-dimensional GEMM reductions. No row sums computed at final step.\"\n  }\n]\n", "exit_code": 0, "status": "completed"}}
{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_43", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/sh -lc \"cat > /tmp/research_history/episode10_derivations.md <<'MD'\n# Exact kernel mechanisms investigated in episode 10\n\nLet K_ij = exp((f_i + g_j - C_ij - shift)/epsilon), r_i = sum_j K_ij and c_j = sum_i K_ij. Each simultaneous half dual update sends the next kernel to\n\nK'_ij = (K_ij sqrt(m/r_i) sqrt(n/c_j))\"'^(epsilon/epsilon'\"').\n\nThe selected annealing schedule quarters epsilon. Thus two in-place squares realize the power without generic pow. Row blocking does not change the operator: each row marginal is local, and column marginals are additive across row blocks. The only differences from the full-tensor implementation are float32 summation order and dispatch. The padded class version can block independent whole class pairs, retaining each matrix's original physical strides and masks.\n\nAt the last retained point step only the reference potential g is needed. The column factor can remain outside the dense kernel update:\n\nlog c'_j = log(sum_i (K_ij sqrt(m/r_i))\"'^q) + (q/2) log(n/c_j), q=epsilon/epsilon'\"'.\n\nSources607/608 apply cache blocks only to the dense term and accumulate columns by GEMM against a two-dimensional ones column. The vector logarithm and original zero-mass logsumexp fallback remain unchanged.\n\nReplacing r=K @ ones_m and c=K.T @ ones_n preserves the same empirical marginal definitions. Two-dimensional right operands intentionally dispatch GEMM; previous episode9 bare GEMV controls were significantly slower. Point and class controls separate row/column dispatch, because their physical layouts differ. No input-dependent lookup or support omission occurs.\n\nSources585/586 use the already-required label matrix L_ij as the addmm input and output. Since C_ij = L_ij + ||a_i||\"'^2/2 + ||b_j||''^2/2 - a_i dot b_j, both norm-first and label-first forms are identical in real arithmetic. They eliminate the separate geometry output allocation and negation pass, while controlling roundoff order. Fresh diagnostic benchmarks do not show improvement for these source variants.\n\nThe coupling controls approximate 1/t about t=1, where t_j=m*v_j*(K.T @ u)_j. Current w=v*(2-t) is first order; controls add half/full (1-t)''^2 or use exact 1/t. A more accurate reciprocal need not make final valuations closer, because other finite-schedule approximations also contribute to the total error. Local30-case controls588–590 confirm that their worst nRMSE increases slightly from the base, so no new quality budget is established.\nMD'", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
