{"type": "item.started", "item": {"id": "item_70", "type": "command_execution", "command": "/bin/sh -lc \"cat >> /tmp/research_history/episode10_derivations.md <<'MD'\n\nExact dense point cost factorization:\n\nA_i = [-x_i (10 entries), ||x_i||\"'^2/2, 1, L[y_i,:] (10 entries)]\nB_j = [r_j (10 entries), 1, ||r_j||''^2/2, one_hot(ry_j) (10 entries)]\n\nThen C=A @ B.T gives the same squared-feature geometry and observed-label cost in22dimensions. Public data and labels are the only inputs to these factors; L is the empirical class-transport result calculated during the current invocation. The factors are created once for the complete candidate/reference arrays and then sliced by the same contiguous1024point batches. Padding unused inner dimensions with zeros changes no exact arithmetic. Homogeneous class distance factors use only12dimensions because no label-cost term is present there.\n\nThis factorization operates on the complete cost, not on the exponential kernel: exp(-C/epsilon) generally has no finite22-dimensional factorization. It therefore retains the original dense kernels and annealing. Its possible gain is eliminating temporary label matrices and dense additions, at the expense of a larger inner dimension in a fast matrix-product implementation.\nMD'", "aggregated_output": "", "exit_code": null, "status": "in_progress"}}
